Explanation:
The correct syntax which is used to access a package element is –
package_name.element_name;
The package name depends on the user and the element name is the name of the element which is to be accessed.

The correct syntax for deleting a procedure is –
DROP PROCEDURE procedure_name;
The DROP & PROCEDURE keyword is used followed by the name of the procedure that has to be deleted.

Question:
Select the correct output for the following PL/SQL code.
DECLARE
   var1 varchar2(20) := 'hello sanfoundry';
BEGIN
   dbms_output.put_line(INITCAP(var1));
END;
/

Select the correct output for the following PL/SQL code.
DECLARE
   var1 varchar2(20) := 'hello sanfoundry';
BEGIN
   dbms_output.put_line(INSTR(var1, ‘s’));
END;
/

Select the correct output for the following PL/SQL code.
DECLARE
   var1 varchar2(30) := '????hello sanfoundry????';
BEGIN
   dbms_output.put_line(TRIM(‘?’ FROM var1));
END;
/

Select the correct output for the following PL/SQL code.
DECLARE
   var1 varchar2(20) := 'hello sanfoundry';
BEGIN
   dbms_output.put_line(SUBSTR (var1, 8, 4));
END;
/

Explanation:
The correct way to declare a variable in PL/SQL is – variable_name

datatype [NOT NULL := VALUE];
Datatype is written after the variable name and the NOT NULL constraint and the value is optional in the declaration part of the variable.

The statement which is used to initialise a variable in PL/SQL is –
variable_name datatype := VALUE;
The datatype is after the variable name and the value is assigned using := (assignment operator).

The statement which is used to take input from the user in PL/SQL is –


variable_name datatype := &variable_name;
The assignment operator (:=) is followed by &variable_name to take input from the user.

